# Build artifacts.  Every pattern here is ANCHORED (leading `/', or a path with
# a slash in it).  An unanchored pattern matches at every depth, and a bare
# `core' once ate 59 files of vendored source in the parent repository -- the
# rule cost a clean clone its contents and nobody noticed until a build failed
# for a reason `git status' could not show.  If you add a rule, anchor it, then
# check `git ls-files' still lists what you expect.

# ---- /build is output ------------------------------------------------------
# Artifacts, not source: everything under it is produced by a build whose every
# part DEPS names, and nothing here is irreplaceable.
#
# This rule does NOT defend against `git merge upstream/master': a merge
# happily creates tracked files at ignored paths.  Upstream is imported BY PATH
# (4c4828f5), and build/ is off the path list -- that, not this line, is what
# keeps the artifacts out.
/build/

# A `make BUILD=build-<something>' staging tree, so a comparison build goes
# beside /build rather than overwriting it.  Anchored, and it cannot match
# /build itself.
/build-*/

# Temporary working space.  Extraction and one-off experiments go here rather
# than in /tmp, so they sit beside the tree they are about.
/scratch/

# ---- 3.5 tree: the host build ---------------------------------------------
/hostbuild/build/
/hostbuild/kobj/
# `dist.py deps' output.  The Makefile writes it to build/mk/<dist>.d, which
# the rule above already covers; this catches a hand-run `dist.py deps > .d',
# which is how a fragment holding 181 absolute paths into a directory layout
# two moves ago came to be tracked and read by nothing.
/hostbuild/.d
# link-kernel.sh builds into kobj.<pid> and publishes into kobj/ by rename, so a
# failed link cannot delete the kernel every other reader is using.
/hostbuild/kobj.*/
/hostbuild/logs/
# net native-build products.  By pattern, not by name: the list was
# maintained a binary at a time and a new client reached a commit before anyone
# noticed.  Everything net builds is an extensionless file next to its source.
/net/*.o
/net/*.a
/net/test/*.o
/net/resolv/*.o
/net/inet/*.o
/net/inet/generic/*.o
/net/[a-z]*
/net/inet/[a-z]*
# A directory matched by the two patterns above is never descended into, so no
# `!' rule inside it is ever consulted -- that hid a whole ported directory and
# a notes file from `git status'.  Un-ignoring the subdirectories themselves is
# what lets the per-directory rules below apply.
!/net/*/
!/net/inet/*/
!/net/*.c
!/net/*.h
!/net/*.s
!/net/Makefile
!/net/include/
!/net/test/
!/net/resolv/
!/net/inet/*.c
!/net/inet/*.h
!/net/inet/*.s
!/net/inet/Makefile
!/net/inet/generic/

# ---- on-target test programs (built by ccz from test/*/) --------------
# build-tests.sh's convention is tests/<name>/<name>.c -> tests/<name>/<name>,
# so one pattern covers every present and future one.
/test/*/[a-z]*
!/test/*/*.c
!/test/*/*.h
!/test/*/*.s
!/test/*/*.py
# A probe written in shell is still a probe, and tests/ascii/check.sh IS the
# ascii test -- but it matched the [a-z]* rule above and so existed in one
# working tree only.
!/test/*/*.sh
!/test/*/Makefile
!/test/*/*cmd*
!/test/cmd/*
!/test/ichan/*
# The test image's /dev table is data, not a built program.
!/test/image/devices

# ---- kboot ----------------------------------------------------------------
/boot/build/

# ---- games and MGR native-build products ----------------------------------
/games/net/hunt/hunt/hunt
/games/net/hunt/huntd/huntd
/games/net/hunt/*/*.o

# ---- generated sources ----------------------------------------------------
# ckcpro.c is what wart (ckwart.c) makes of the ckcpro.w state-machine
# description, and build-comms.sh remakes it whenever it is absent or older
# than the .w.  A tracked copy is a second answer to the same question: the
# build overwrites it, so any edit made to it -- a copyright notice included --
# is lost the next time the .w moves.  The source is the .w.
/comms/ckermit/ckcpro.c
# tables.c is what games/bsd/adventure/host/dumper.c.host makes of glorkz by
# running the original startup scan, and build-games.sh remakes it whenever it
# is absent or older than glorkz or anything in host/.  The message file the
# same run writes goes straight to the staged tree.  The source is glorkz.
/games/bsd/adventure/tables.c

# ---- make deps ------------------------------------------------------------
# Where `make deps' unpacks the pinned third-party release binaries DEPS names
# (the emulator).  Our own repositories are cloned as SIBLINGS, outside this
# tree, so nothing else needs a rule here.
/deps/

# ---- python ---------------------------------------------------------------
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# A `make BUILD=build-<something>' staging tree under hostbuild, the same
# thing /build-*/ covers at the top level.  That rule is anchored and never
# matched here, so 4456 objects and staged roots were committed once.  The
# trailing slash matters: build-*.sh are the build SCRIPTS and are tracked.
hostbuild/build-*/
